---
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="ShadowADJ — Product Specification & About">
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12 leading-relaxed">
    <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-full border border-cyan/30 bg-cyan/10 px-3 py-1 text-xs font-semibold text-cyan">
      Product Specification & Architecture
    </div>

    <h1 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-bold text-white sm:text-4xl">
      ShadowADJ: Real-Time AI Assistance Monitoring for Competitive Debate
    </h1>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-8 text-white/80">
      <div class="glass p-6">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-cyan">1. What is ShadowADJ?</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-white/70">
          ShadowAdj is a real-time monitoring assistant for competitive debate adjudicators. It runs quietly in the background during a live round and analyses speech for signals that may indicate undisclosed AI assistance. It does not replace the adjudicator, decide whether someone cheated, or determine the winner. Its purpose is to give the adjudicator additional evidence while they focus on judging the debate.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="glass p-6">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-cyan">2. The Problem</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-white/70">
          Competitive debate depends on speakers competing under the same rules. However, an adjudicator is already busy flowing arguments, tracking rebuttals, evaluating mechanisms and impacts, comparing clashes, assessing delivery, and preparing an oral adjudication. They cannot realistically act as an AI investigator at the same time.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-white/70">
          This creates a particular risk for honest speakers. Students may spend months preparing and significant amounts of money on registration, travel and accommodation for national and international competitions. If another speaker gains an undisclosed AI advantage, the honest speaker may lose a round, ranking, qualification, prize, or opportunity without the adjudicator even realizing that an unfair advantage existed.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="glass p-6">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-cyan">3. Who Does It Help?</h2>
        <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm text-white/70">
          <li><strong>Honest debaters</strong> who compete without prohibited or undisclosed AI assistance.</li>
          <li><strong>Students travelling and paying</strong> to participate in high-level national and international tournaments.</li>
          <li><strong>Adjudicators</strong> who should be able to focus on arguments and clashes instead of second-guessing AI use.</li>
          <li><strong>Tournament organisers</strong> who want a practical way to support fair-play rules.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="glass p-6">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-cyan">4. How It Works & Pipeline</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-white/70">
          During a debate, ShadowAdj receives live audio and processes it in short segments:
        </p>
        <div class="mt-4 grid gap-2.5 sm:grid-cols-2 text-xs font-mono">
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-black/40 p-3">1. Live debate audio → speech segmentation & speaker ID</div>
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-black/40 p-3">2. Speech → real-time low-latency transcription</div>
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-black/40 p-3">3. Audio + transcript → speech-pattern & linguistic features</div>
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-black/40 p-3">4. Features → multi-signal risk engine</div>
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-black/40 p-3 sm:col-span-2">5. Signals → simple, non-disruptive alert for adjudicator HUD</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="glass p-6">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-cyan">5. What Does ShadowADJ Look For?</h2>
        <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm text-white/70">
          <li>Changes in speaking rate (WPM), pauses, pitch and delivery patterns.</li>
          <li>Sudden shifts in vocabulary variety (MATTR), sentence structure, or linguistic complexity.</li>
          <li>Unexpected shifts between prepared and spontaneous responses.</li>
          <li>Semantic or structural anomalies in arguments and rebuttals.</li>
          <li>Verbatim unattributed external source matches.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-amber/80">
          No single signal is treated as proof of AI use. A combination of signals can instead indicate that a particular moment is worth the adjudicator's attention.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="glass p-6">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-cyan">6. What the Adjudicator Sees</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-white/70">
          The interface is intentionally simple so that it does not distract from the round. Instead of showing an aggressive 'AI DETECTED' warning, ShadowAdj shows a low, elevated, or high-risk signal, together with a short explanation and relevant timestamp.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-3 rounded-lg border border-amber/30 bg-amber/10 p-3 font-mono text-xs text-amber">
          Example: "23:14 — Elevated signal: significant deviation from established speech pattern & unattributed source match."
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="glass p-6">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-cyan">7. The Human Stays in Control</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-white/70">
          ShadowAdj never decides that a speaker cheated and never decides who wins. An alert is evidence for consideration, not a verdict. This is essential because nervousness, accents, fast speaking, strong preparation, or a difficult rebuttal can all create legitimate changes in speech.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm font-semibold text-white">
          The system monitors. The speaker competes. The adjudicator decides.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="glass p-6">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-cyan">8. Core Value Proposition</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-white/70">
          ShadowAdj protects speakers who play by the rules while giving adjudicators confidence that they can focus on the debate itself rather than constantly wondering whether an undisclosed AI advantage is affecting the round.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
